An application crash handler hides the crash from everything outside the process.

A fatal signal goes to the handler the application installed, which is correct,
and is also the end of any hope of debugging it: the handler prints its own
message and exits cleanly, so there is no core, coredumpctl has nothing, and
scripts/core-guest-stack.py has nothing to read.

LibreOffice does exactly this. Its FIRST run against a new profile dies with
"Unspecified Application Error" and zero windows, and every instrument in the
tree was silent: no exception, no unrecognised selector, and SAL_LOG produces
nothing at all because nixpkgs ships the release build with logging compiled
out. The only trace of what happened was one line in the daemon log,

    sigexc: will forward signal to app handler (0x7334D03B41A0)

CIDER_SIGNAL_NO_APP_HANDLER=1 takes the DEFAULT effect for the four signals
that mean the process is broken (SEGV, BUS, ILL, FPE), so the kernel writes a
core and the stack can be resolved. With it set, the same run produced a core
and one line of answer:

    tid 3  libskialo.dylib+0x49936b
           skwindow::internal::MetalWindowContext::checkDestroyShared (+43)

The switch changes nothing unless it is set, and it is read ONCE at setup
rather than in the handler, where opening a file would be a new way to crash.
The environment is read through /proc/self/environ rather than getenv, because
this file is below libc and there is no environ pointer here yet.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/signal/sigexc.c	2026-08-16 03:05:31.135655519 +0200
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/signal/sigexc.c	2026-08-16 03:05:31.118028103 +0200
